# Topoi and stacks: the open problems, not stated

Section « Topoi and stacks » of the note on the open problems of the Montpellier notes
(`docs/problems/article.tex`): problems p:noncoh, p:fol, p:pic, p:domram. None of them can be
stated faithfully with Mathlib and the Stacks library; for each, what is missing. A first-order
part of p:noncoh is proved in `OpenProblems/TopoiStacksNoncoh.lean`.

## Not formalised

* p:noncoh (folder 161-2, pp. 39, 41–42, *Algèbre universelle*, [c. 1963–1973]): the classifying
  topoi of the geometric theories of rings with irreducible spectrum and of rings with
  zero-dimensional spectrum are not coherent; do they have enough points? Status of the points
  question: answered (yes) by the edition (finding
  `161-2-irreducible-and-zero-dimensional-not-coherent`, and the note), by an argument of the
  edition not checked by a specialist. Both theories are geometric theories over the countable
  signature of rings, with countably many axioms, each with at most countable disjunctions
  (« `ab` nilpotent ⟹ `a` or `b` nilpotent » as a disjunction over the exponents; « every
  element is π-regular » as `⋁ₙ ∃ b, aⁿ = aⁿ⁺¹ b`). So each classifying topos is the topos of
  sheaves on a countable site with pullbacks for a topology generated by countably many sieves,
  and has enough points by Theorem 6.2.4 of M. Makkai, G. E. Reyes, *First Order Categorical
  Logic. Model-Theoretical Methods in the Theory of Topoi and Related Categories*, Lecture Notes
  in Mathematics 611, Springer, 1977, doi:10.1007/BFb0066201 (restated as Corollary 3.3.10 of
  I. Di Liberti, M. Rogers, *Topoi with enough points*, arXiv:2403.15338). Non-coherence: as
  stated in the note. Missing: geometric theories and their classifying topoi, coherent topoi,
  and points of a topos. The Stacks library has morphisms of topoi (`MorphismOfTopoi`), not
  these. Proved (`OpenProblems.TopoiStacksNoncoh`): neither class of commutative rings, the
  rings with prime nilradical and the rings of Krull dimension `≤ 0` (or `= 0`), is the class of
  models of a first-order theory in the language of rings
  (`not_isAxiomatizable_nilradical_isPrime`, `not_isAxiomatizable_krullDimLE_zero`,
  `not_isAxiomatizable_ringKrullDim_eq_zero`), by Łoś's theorem; so neither theory has the same
  set-theoretic models as a coherent quotient of the theory of rings, which is what lies behind
  the page's remark that the presenting topologies are not quasi-compact. This does not prove
  that the classifying topoi are not coherent for every presentation. The points argument is not
  formalised.
* p:fol (folder 119, pp. 144–145, March 1986): an equivalence between spaces with a local
  equivalence relation (locally open, locally connected fibres) and locally open geometric
  morphisms with locally connected fibres from `Top(X)` to a « multiplicité topologique ».
  Status: open (the functors are in SGA 4 IV 9.8.3 and in Kock and Moerdijk; no equivalence
  found). Missing: local equivalence relations on spaces, their quotient topoi, locally open and
  locally connected geometric morphisms, étendues; and the page's third condition is partly
  illegible.
* p:pic (folder 134-1, p. 5, letter to Deligne, 27 August 1974; withdrawn in part in a letter to
  Breen of 5 February 1975): is every homomorphism `M → ₂N` of abelian sheaves on a topos the
  symmetry invariant of a Picard stack pinned by `M` and `N`? Status: open; the 1974 « universal »
  argument rests on a miscalculation by the 1975 letter's account. Missing: stacks over a topos,
  Picard stacks and their symmetry invariant, and `Ext³` of abelian sheaves on a topos as the home
  of the obstruction (Mathlib has `Ext` in abelian categories, but not the Picard-stack side).
* p:domram (folder 29, p. 197, *Groupe fondamental*, 1959–1969): morphisms of « domaines de
  ramification » (stacks of multigaloisian categories over the étale site with a geometric
  realisation, axioms R1, R2) are fully faithful. Status: open (Proposition 6 of the page, without
  proof; nothing found). Missing: stacks of categories over the étale site, multigaloisian
  categories, and the axioms R1–R2 themselves, which the edition has not verified.

